WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:29.200
This is Euglena ehrenbergii, and as you can
see, it is a bit of a shapeshifter.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:36.120
It’s not just changing its shape though…it’s moving.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:44.080
Across the surface of this unicellular eukaryote,
a series of strips made out of protein run

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:51.620
the length of the cell, working with a system
of molecular tubes to drive this cellular

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:56.720
contortionist around in a form of movement
known as metaboly.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:05.540
Those strips create a ridged surface along the plasma membrane called the pellicle, this is the

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:14.880
structure that unites the otherwise incredibly diverse set of organisms called euglenoids.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:23.360
Euglenoids are mostly found in freshwater,
where many species get their food by engulfing

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:27.000
it through phagocytosis.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:34.560
Millions, or maybe even a billion or so years
ago, the thing that got engulfed was a photosynthetic

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.590
green algae.

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:39.780
This marks a major division.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:44.240
Many euglenoids, like this peranema, don’t have chloroplasts.

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:51.070
Instead, these non-photosynthetic species
rely on feeding mechanisms like phagocytosis

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:52.700
or osmosis.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:57.960
But the euglenoids we’re going to talk about
today are the ones whose ancestors not only

00:01:57.979 --> 00:02:04.560
ingested the green algae, they took on the
algae’s chloroplast and the rest of the

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:12.230
photosynthetic apparatus, evolving the ability
to make their own food in addition to consuming

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:14.800
nutrients from their environment.

00:02:18.769 --> 00:02:25.440
As we mentioned earlier, the common link between
euglenoids is that striped surface called

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:27.120
the pellicle.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:32.269
Other than that though, euglenoids can look
pretty different from each other.

00:02:32.269 --> 00:02:38.569
The most common shape among Euglenoids is
this sort of elongated almond shape, where

00:02:38.569 --> 00:02:42.120
the end comes to a tapered point.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:48.659
Then there are these Monomorphina pyrum, whose
bodies are more rounded and have a clearer

00:02:48.659 --> 00:02:51.779
tail coming out at one end.

00:02:51.779 --> 00:02:58.310
You can see Monomorphina pyrum here as well,
along with Phacus tortus.

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:04.959
Almost all Phacus species have this sort of
flat, diamond body, resembling a leaf, and

00:03:04.959 --> 00:03:15.180
some—like this Phacus helicoides—can twist that leaf into an amazing and beautiful corkscrew-like structure.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:23.769
Phacus species do have a pellicle, but they are rigid and can’t actually engage in metaboly,

00:03:23.769 --> 00:03:29.110
that shape-shifting method of locomotion we saw in the beginning that is so common to

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:33.980
euglenoids that it’s also called euglenoid
movement.

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:40.019
In general, the flexibility of euglenoids
corresponds to the number of protein strips

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:41.520
in its pellicles.

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:47.279
Species that are less rigid and thus more
likely to use metaboly have more strips, while

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:51.020
more rigid species have fewer strips.

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:56.560
So you might be wondering how these rigid
species get around if they can’t rely on

00:03:56.579 --> 00:03:59.909
wiggling their bodies to different places.

00:03:59.909 --> 00:04:03.609
The answer is simple: flagella.

00:04:03.609 --> 00:04:09.379
All photosynthetic euglenoids have two or
more of those hair-like structures that they

00:04:09.379 --> 00:04:13.949
use to move, even if they are capable of metaboly.

00:04:13.949 --> 00:04:19.560
The flagella are a little hard to see…if
you squint, you might see them here towards

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:26.140
the front end of the cell, which is also where
you’ll find that distinct reddish spot that

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:28.430
looks kind of like an eye.

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:35.130
That red spot is, appropriately enough, the eyespot, though it doesn’t actually see

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:41.659
Instead, the eyespot works as a sort of “shading device”, that along with photoreceptive

00:04:41.659 --> 00:04:47.620
proteins at the base of the flagella, tells
the cell where it can find light to drive

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.380
photosynthesis.

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:57.940
Now, despite their ancient green algae origins, the chloroplasts in photosynthetic euglenoids

00:04:57.949 --> 00:05:05.340
are a bit different from their ancestral counterparts,
particularly in how they store their energy.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:10.980
Green algae make a starch that is stored in the chloroplast for their later energetic

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:14.520
needs, because the sun never shines all day.

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:19.430
but euglenoids produce a different
carbohydrate stock called paramylon that is

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:25.200
kept in the cytoplasm of the cell instead of in the chloroplast.

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:30.780
Different euglenoids keep their paramylon
stored in different shapes, ranging from small

00:05:30.790 --> 00:05:33.870
discs to larger rods.

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:42.180
As this euglenoid turns, you can actually
see its paramylon bodies. It’s those oval ring structures.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:49.320
And when the cell uses the paramylon for energy,
those rings will become thinner.

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:55.670
Of course, as we’ve seen in other episodes,
things in the microcosmos can get tough, so

00:05:55.670 --> 00:06:00.050
some euglenoids build their own defenses.

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:01.800
This is Trachelmonas.

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:08.080
And I should say here that I am fumbling through some of these pronunciations so don't take them as gospel.

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:12.140
It's encased in a protective structure called the

00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:17.560
lorica, a sugary matrix that the cell produces
when its young.

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:22.580
The lorica starts out as a softer, more flexible
covering.

00:06:22.599 --> 00:06:29.440
But over time, it picks up minerals like iron
and becomes increasingly solid.

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:34.990
You can tell that compared to the other very
green euglenoids we’ve shown, this little

00:06:34.990 --> 00:06:37.580
guy is more on the yellowish side.

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:43.900
That color actually comes from those embedded
minerals.

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:51.980
These Euglena sanguinea are demonstrating
another use for sticky encasements under stress.

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:56.419
These cells have entered what is called the palmelloid stage.

00:06:56.419 --> 00:07:04.000
On the right side of this clip, you can see
four cells enclosed in a clear membrane, but

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:10.400
when this little clique was started, it was
originally just one cell that had lost its flagella

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:14.620
and enclosed itself in a capsule of its own
making.

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:21.030
In time, that one cell divided into two, and
then those two cells divided into four.

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:28.120
When conditions are right, these cells
can leave the capsule and reform their flagella.

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:36.830
Euglenoids have had a very, very long time
to evolve, and as we’ve seen, the things

00:07:36.830 --> 00:07:43.819
that they have evolved into are diverse—so
diverse that, combined with the varied shape-shifting

00:07:43.820 --> 00:07:53.320
abilities of its member species, euglenoids have proven challenging, both to identify and to classify.

00:07:53.320 --> 00:08:00.060
In the past few centuries, attempts to categorize
them have resulted in their inclusion with

00:08:00.070 --> 00:08:08.620
animals, with plants, and with protists, as well declarations of their own kingdom.

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:15.249
The microscope is a powerful tool, but this is where molecular tools like gene sequencing

00:08:15.249 --> 00:08:22.949
take over, helping scientists better understand just how the species of this vast group are

00:08:22.949 --> 00:08:27.550
related to each other and to everything else.

00:08:27.550 --> 00:08:34.710
And even as these threads of evolution get
untangled and old questions get answered,

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:41.940
there will only be more questions to ask and opportunities to wonder.

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:48.920
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:08:48.920 --> 00:08:52.780
If you want to see more from our Master of Microscopes, James Weiss,

00:08:52.780 --> 00:08:55.880
check out Jam and Germs on Instagram.

00:08:55.880 --> 00:09:02.600
And if you want to come back to see more of the beautiful, weird world of the micro

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:07.400
there's probably a subscribe button that you can click on.

